<!--
  App.1 Figure 2 (map-type ADS for roundabouts). All lengths in sw.
  Stated on the figure: ring D = 24 sw and d = 14 sw (so 5 sw wide); "Min12 sw" on the ahead and
  both side exits; "Min10 sw" on the approach; side tiles "2.0 sw" outside the ring's vertical
  tangents (legendToRingSide).
  Measured from the Figure 2 vector drawing (1 sw = 3.345 pt from D = 24): the exits and the
  approach are dimensioned from about the ring's tangent on the arm axis (ahead 13.3 sw drawn,
  approach dimension line 10.0 sw starting 0.5 sw above the tangent), so both minimums are taken
  from the tangent as in UK TSM Chapter 7 Figure 5-13. The cut-out is a radial wedge with edges at
  about 60 and 31.5 degrees below the horizontal: 30 degrees from the approach axis, 30 degrees wide.
  Not stated in App.1: a minimum cut-out angle (the measured 30 is used, so no smaller cut-out is
  ever drawn) and the gap from the ring to a border where no arm leaves that side (Figure 2 shows
  all three exits; 4 sw is borrowed from UK TSM Chapter 7 Figure 5-13). These are named in
  'provisional' and every Uganda roundabout raises an Info diagnostic quoting it.
-->
<roundaboutSection innerDiameter="14.0" outerDiameter="24.0"
                   exitMinLength="12.0" approachMinLength="10.0"
                   cutOutFromApproachDeg="30.0" cutOutAngleDeg="30.0" cutOutMinAngleDeg="30.0"
                   legendToRingSide="2.0" ringToBorder="4.0"
                   provisional="App.1 Figure 2: the cut-out (30 degrees from the approach, 30 degrees wide) and the arm-length base (the ring tangent) are measured from the drawing; App.1 states no minimum cut-out angle; the 4 sw ring-to-border gap where no arm leaves is borrowed from UK TSM Chapter 7 Figure 5-13"/>
